<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=quantum-mechanics">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quantum mechanics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Quantum physics, Quantum theory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quantum mechanics is the physical theory that describes the behaviour of matter and light at and below the scale of atoms; it arose from early theories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a>'s hypothesis of 1900 that energy is radiated and absorbed in discrete &quot;quanta&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> used the idea in 1905 to explain the photoelectric effect, and modern quantum mechanics was born in 1925, when Werner Heisenberg, Max Born and Pascual Jordan developed matrix mechanics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-schrodinger">Erwin Schrödinger</a> invented wave mechanics. Its applications include the laser, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-resonance-imaging">magnetic resonance imaging</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics.jpg" alt="Quantum mechanics" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Couprie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solvay_conference_1927.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Quantum mechanics arose gradually from attempts to explain observations that classical physics could not account for. Scientific inquiry into the wave nature of light began in the 17th and 18th centuries, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hooke">Robert Hooke</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonhard-euler">Leonhard Euler</a> proposed a wave theory of light, and in 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-young-scientist">Thomas Young</a> described the double-slit experiment, which did much to bring about the general acceptance of that theory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a>'s solution to the black-body radiation problem and the correspondence between energy and frequency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>'s paper on the photoelectric effect are the best-known early steps. These attempts, now called the old quantum theory, led in the mid-1920s to the full theory, developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr">Niels Bohr</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-schrodinger">Erwin Schrödinger</a>, Werner Heisenberg, Max Born, Paul Dirac and others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The theory describes matter and light at and below the scale of atoms, where classical physics is insufficient. Quantum systems have bound states quantised to discrete values of energy, momentum and angular momentum, whereas classical systems can take them continuously. Measurements show characteristics of both particles and waves, and the uncertainty principle sets limits on how accurately the value of a physical quantity can be predicted before it is measured. In one formalism the wave function gives probability amplitudes for what measurements of a particle's energy, momentum and other properties may yield, and its change in time is described by the Schrödinger equation.</p><p>Several mathematically equivalent formulations exist. Dirac's transformation theory unifies the two earliest, Heisenberg's matrix mechanics and Schrödinger's wave mechanics, and Richard Feynman's path integral formulation treats an amplitude as a sum over all possible paths between an initial and a final state. Classical mechanics can be derived from quantum mechanics as an approximation valid at ordinary scales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Quantum mechanics has been verified experimentally to an extremely high degree of accuracy. Quantum electrodynamics, the first complete quantum field theory, agrees with experiment to within 1 part in 10 to the power 12 for the magnetic properties of an electron. Solid-state physics and materials science depend on it, and its applications include quantum chemistry, superconducting magnets, light-emitting diodes, the optical amplifier, the laser, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microprocessor">microprocessor</a>, and imaging such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-resonance-imaging">magnetic resonance imaging</a> and electron microscopy.</p><p>Its counter-intuitive results have provoked philosophical debate since the start, centred on probability, wavefunction collapse and the measurement problem. The views of Bohr, Heisenberg and others are grouped as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> interpretation, which treats the probabilistic nature of the theory as final rather than temporary. Steven Weinberg judged that there is no entirely satisfactory interpretation. Its formalism and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">general relativity</a> contradict each other, and reconciling them has been a major goal of physics since the 20th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quantum mechanics” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It helped found <b>quantum theory</b> and earned him the Nobel Prize.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
